      "text": "Let's start by recalling what we know: the set consists of three numbers, and the mean (average) of these numbers is 4. We are also told that the median of the set is 4.\n\nGiven that the median is 4, we can infer that one of the numbers is 4 because the median is the middle value when the numbers are arranged in ascending order. This means that our set of numbers looks like some arrangement of: [small number, number at position, large number], where None of these numbers are 1.\n\nTo ensure the mean is 4, let's multiply the possible three-digit number 400 (which is 4*100) by 3:\n\n3 * 400 = 1200\n\nSince the smallest number only satisfies the fact that it's smaller than all others when there's more than one smallest number (to satisfy all the conditions), let's consider the smallest number (other than 1) to be 4. This will serve as the starting point since known median and mean leads us to believe the number being 4 is the second number.\n\nNow we just need to fill out the rest and calculate the range. We already know two of the factors and we make their product, 1200. If 1 is set in the smallest spot, we now have:\n\n[num, presum-num, 4 where num is a number greater than or equal to 4]\n\nNumber (presum-num) determined: it is 8\n\nSo.\n3 * 8 = 24\nWhere we actually get 1200 (1*400) if 400 is substituted for num.\n\nFinally, we compute the range, subtracting the smallest number from the highest:\n\n1200 - 1 = 1199\n\nThus, the range of the sets of numbers corresponding to smallest number 1, median 4, and mean 4, is 1199. The answer is\n\\boxed{1199}.",
